
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Two more questions and I'll have enough to present a design.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what the rule is for [3mwhen[0m a reminder fires relative to the due date. This shapes the task model and whether
  there's a settings surface at all.

  - [1mOverdue-only[0m — a task notifies when it passes its due time, never before. No lead-time concept, no settings screen,
    one field on the task. Downside: it only ever tells you you're late, which is the least useful moment.
  - [1mGlobal lead time + overdue[0m — one app-wide setting ("remind me 1 hour before"), applied to every task, plus the
    overdue alert. One setting, one extra field to store, covers most real use.
  - [1mPer-task lead time[0m — each task carries its own offset. Most flexible; costs a control in the task editor, a default
    to fall back to, and more surface in both the model and the tests.

  [1mMy recommendation: global lead time + overdue.[0m It gets you the alert [3mbefore[0m you're late — the whole point — without a
  per-task control that most tasks would leave at the default. Adding a per-task override later is purely additive: the
  field defaults to "use global," so nothing stored today has to change.

  Worth noting these are cheap to change [3mexcept[0m per-task, which once shipped means stored tasks carry an offset field
  you have to keep migrating.

